<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Release. Info-Debug.plist is this file plus an App Transport Security exception for
	     local cleartext; Config.xcconfig selects between them with INFOPLIST_FILE[config=Debug].
	     Keys added here must be added there too — there is no shared base, because a plist
	     cannot include another one. Everything below is a $() reference rather than a literal,
	     so version and identity still come from the target.

	     Authored rather than generated: GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES cannot carry custom keys,
	     and this file lives beside the .xcodeproj because Rascal/Rascal/ is a synchronized
	     folder group where a stray plist is also copied as a resource. -->
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<!-- **The name under the icon, and literal on purpose.** `CFBundleName` below was
	     `$(PRODUCT_NAME)`, which resolves to the *target* name — "Rascal" — and a target name is a
	     build artefact, not a product. Setting this explicitly is what renamed the app — to Dogear on
	     Aug 12, 2026, to Vellum on Aug 17, 2026 and to Pocket Chronicle on Aug 27, 2026
	     without touching the target, the Swift module, the bundle id or the App Group, none of
	     which a parent ever sees and all of which cost signing work to move.

	     **This key and `CFBundleName` below stopped being the same string on Aug 27, 2026.** The
	     home-screen label truncates around eleven characters and "Pocket Chronicle" is sixteen, so
	     the label is **"Chronicle"** and the name iOS speaks in its own sentences is **"Pocket
	     Chronicle"**. Chrome, Maps and Photos all do this. It is deliberate — do not "fix" it.

	     It was never sufficient on its own, and the note on `CFBundleName` below is the other
	     half of the rename.

	     The widget and the share extension carry the same string in their own plists, and so does
	     Info-Debug.plist. **All four have to agree** — this file, Info-Debug.plist,
	     RascalWidget-Info.plist and RascalShare-Info.plist — and since Aug 23, 2026 they have to
	     agree on both name keys, not just this one. ~~Three of these comments said "all
	     three"~~ and undercounted the debug variant, which is the copy of this file most likely to
	     drift precisely because it is maintained by hand. iOS groups the widget gallery by the
	     app's display name, and a widget reading one name under a heading reading something else
	     is worse than either alone.

	     **Since Aug 27, 2026 "agree" means per key, not across keys.** All four must read
	     "Chronicle" for this key and "Pocket Chronicle" for `CFBundleName`. What must never happen
	     is one plist disagreeing with another on the same key. -->
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Chronicle</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<!-- **Literal too, and the note above was half the story until Aug 23, 2026.**
	     `CFBundleDisplayName` is the name under the icon. `CFBundleName` is the name iOS uses in
	     *its own* sentences about the app, and the two are not interchangeable: this is the key
	     `ASWebAuthenticationSession` reads for the *X Wants to Use "accounts.google.com" to Sign In*
	     alert that opens every Google sign-in, which is one of the first things a new parent sees.
	     Left at `$(PRODUCT_NAME)` it resolved to the target name, so that parent was asked to trust
	     "Rascal" — a name the product has not carried since Aug 12, 2026 and that appears nowhere
	     else on screen — on the one dialog whose entire job is to establish that the app asking is
	     the app they installed.

	     Determined by experiment rather than from the documentation, which is not specific: a
	     throwaway simulator app with the three candidates set to three different strings
	     (`CFBundleDisplayName`, `CFBundleName`, and the executable name) rendered the
	     `CFBundleName` one. ~~**Both keys are literal now and both move together on a rename.**~~
	     — **struck Aug 27, 2026.** Both are still literal and both still move on a rename, but they
	     no longer move to the *same string*: this one is "Pocket Chronicle", the display name is
	     "Chronicle". A rename that substitutes one name into both keys is wrong in a way the test
	     suite will not catch unless it checks each key separately, which is why
	     `GoogleSignInTests` now pins them per key rather than in one loop. -->
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Pocket Chronicle</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_PACKAGE_TYPE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>

	<!-- The app's only cryptography is HTTPS to its own API, which is the standard exemption.
	     Declared here rather than answered in the browser because App Store Connect asks on
	     every single upload otherwise, and a build sits unavailable to testers until someone
	     notices the question. The answer does not change between builds, so it belongs in the
	     bundle. If a future version ever ships its own encryption — not TLS, its own — this
	     becomes true and export paperwork follows. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<!-- Xcode injects this only when it generates the plist, and GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE is NO
	     here (a generated plist cannot carry the custom API_* keys below). Without it the
	     archive uploads and is rejected by App Store Connect with error 90713, which names the
	     key but not the reason it is absent. -->
	<key>CFBundleIconName</key>
	<string>AppIcon</string>

	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<true/>
		<key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
		<dict/>
	</dict>
	<key>UIApplicationSupportsIndirectInputEvents</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~iphone</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Shown verbatim in the system prompt the first time each is touched. Written as a
	     sentence a parent would actually understand — what is taken, and where it goes. -->
	<key>PHPhotoLibraryPreventAutomaticLimitedAccessAlert</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Pocket Chronicle reads the dates on your photos and how many there are, so it can suggest months worth writing about. The pictures themselves stay on your phone — nothing is uploaded, and nothing is added to the archive unless you pick it.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Save the original, full-quality video back to your photos. The archive keeps a smaller copy so it stays affordable to store for eighteen years.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Take a photo or video for today's prompt without leaving the app.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Record your child's voice for the archive — the entries you will be most glad to have in twenty years.</string>

	<!-- How the widget opens the app. `childarchive://assignment/<uuid>` and
	     `childarchive://today`, parsed by WidgetDeepLink. Named from the bundle id rather than
	     the product, because a scheme a parent has already tapped is as permanent as a bundle id.

	     **That call paid off on Aug 12, 2026**, when the product was named Dogear, again on Aug 17,
	     2026 when it became Vellum, and a third time on Aug 27, 2026 when it became Pocket
	     Chronicle: the scheme did
	     not have to move, so nothing already tapped broke. Leave it `childarchive` — it is an
	     identifier, and renaming it now would buy nothing and cost every existing deep link.
	     Not written as a $() reference either, because it is not a build configuration: pointing
	     the app at another host must not change how the widget reaches it. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.travisterry.childarchive.widget-deeplink</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>childarchive</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- Google's OAuth callback. Unlike the deep-link scheme above, this one IS a $()
		     reference: it is the reversed client id, so pointing the app at a different Google
		     project has to change it, and a scheme that disagrees with GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID means
		     the browser closes onto nothing and the app never hears back. -->
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.travisterry.childarchive.google-oauth</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>$(GOOGLE_REVERSED_CLIENT_ID)</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!-- Read with Bundle.main.object(forInfoDictionaryKey:). Values come from Config.xcconfig,
	     so no base URL and no UUID appears in Swift source.

	     **DEV_LOGIN_EMAIL and DEV_CHILD_ID are in Info-Debug.plist and deliberately not here.**
	     This is the one place the two files are allowed to differ, and the divergence is the fix
	     rather than an oversight: the dev-login path — the welcome screen's button,
	     OnboardingSession.signInWithDevLogin, AppSession.signIn, APIClient.devLogin and AppConfig
	     — is #if DEBUG now, so nothing in a Release binary can read these keys, and shipping the
	     fixture account's address and child id in every App Store build to be read by nothing is
	     a gift to whoever is reading the bundle. Anything a Release build actually reads still has
	     to be added to both files. -->
	<key>API_BASE_URL</key>
	<string>$(API_BASE_URL)</string>
	<key>GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID</key>
	<string>$(GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID)</string>
	<key>GOOGLE_REVERSED_CLIENT_ID</key>
	<string>$(GOOGLE_REVERSED_CLIENT_ID)</string>
	<key>TERMS_URL</key>
	<string>$(TERMS_URL)</string>
	<key>PRIVACY_URL</key>
	<string>$(PRIVACY_URL)</string>
</dict>
</plist>
